59087689-9942-40f4-90a5-7fa820fa5f3c	392e146e-92d3-486c-a4f3-efa54a628127	0b29756f-eb60-4ed6-bb8d-a53a76219996	live_move_1_0570e83b	move	misconception_demolition	\N	{"id": "live_move_1_0570e83b", "type": "misconception_demolition", "phase": "active", "title": "Where do plants get their food?", "blockId": "live_block_1_7024ea43", "content": "{\\"schemaVersion\\":\\"petros.render.v2\\",\\"title\\":\\"Where do plants get their food?\\",\\"type\\":\\"misconception_demolition\\",\\"misconception\\":\\"Plants take food directly from the soil.\\",\\"commitmentPrompt\\":\\"Quickly type your short answer (one sentence): do plants get their food from the soil, or do they make it themselves? Say which and a few words why.\\",\\"correction\\":\\"Plants make (assemble) their own sugar — glucose — from carbon dioxide and water, using energy from light in a process called photosynthesis. Roots absorb water and minerals from the soil, but those are raw materials and support; the actual stored ‘food’ molecules are built inside the leaves using light energy and carbon from the air.\\",\\"evidence\\":[\\"Experiment: A plant kept without light cannot make new leaf sugars and eventually weakens, even if its roots are in rich soil.\\",\\"Observation: Green leaves contain chlorophyll and structures (chloroplasts) that capture light energy to drive the chemical build-up of glucose from CO2 and H2O.\\",\\"Curriculum source: Stage 4 fixture — correction anchor: Plants assemble glucose from carbon dioxide and water using light energy.\\"]}", "startedAtMs": 14520, "completedAtMs": 14520, "sourceAnchors": [], "decisionFactors": ["Topic profile lists 'soil-food' as a high-priority misconception.", "Opening pattern recommends starting with misconception demolition.", "Session is a short revise session (time-limited) so a focused corrective move is efficient."], "artifactProposed": false, "expectedEvidence": "Learner will acknowledge the correction (e.g., agree or restate that plants make food using light, CO2 and water) or answer a quick follow-up that shows understanding.", "firstContentAtMs": 14520, "studentSafeReason": "This move targets a common, harmless misconception and uses corrective instruction — safe and suitable for a confident-but-wrong learner.", "studentConsentRequired": false}	2026-08-09 18:35:00.905796+03
867b126d-e3e6-4fe5-8546-f8017d9a8f5b	392e146e-92d3-486c-a4f3-efa54a628127	0b29756f-eb60-4ed6-bb8d-a53a76219996	live_move_2_8549ac8f	move	goal_frame	\N	{"id": "live_move_2_8549ac8f", "type": "goal_frame", "phase": "opening", "title": "Short shared learning goal", "blockId": "live_block_2_20de39f2", "content": "{\\"schemaVersion\\":\\"petros.render.v2\\",\\"title\\":\\"Short shared learning goal\\",\\"type\\":\\"goal_frame\\",\\"goal\\":\\"In the next 5 minutes, explain and apply the basic photosynthesis equation: identify the reactants (CO2, H2O) and the main product (glucose) and show one simple example of its use.\\",\\"timeEstimateMinutes\\":5,\\"checkpoints\\":[\\"State the chemical equation for photosynthesis and name reactants and products.\\",\\"Give a one-sentence application or example using the equation (e.g., how a plant makes glucose from CO2 and water).\\"]}", "startedAtMs": 29880, "completedAtMs": 29880, "sourceAnchors": [], "decisionFactors": ["Topic profile recommends goal_frame after misconception_demolition as the opening pattern.", "Session is time-limited (900s budget) so a brief, shared outcome is efficient.", "A cheap commitment reveals learner readiness and orients subsequent diagram/explanation moves."], "artifactProposed": false, "expectedEvidence": "Learner gives a brief commitment (one-sentence) about what they will be able to do after 5 minutes (e.g., restate the photosynthesis equation or apply it to a simple example) or attempts a one-sentence application.", "firstContentAtMs": 29880, "studentSafeReason": "Framing a concrete, timeboxed outcome focuses the short revision session and gives the learner a low-effort commitment — safe and appropriate for a confident-but-wrong learner after a corrective move.", "studentConsentRequired": false}	2026-08-09 18:35:00.905796+03
c3799c3f-650c-4bdc-94b2-ef0829c4ec8c	392e146e-92d3-486c-a4f3-efa54a628127	0b29756f-eb60-4ed6-bb8d-a53a76219996	live_move_3_c3cf2c6a	move	diagram	\N	{"id": "live_move_3_c3cf2c6a", "type": "diagram", "phase": "explaining", "title": "Photosynthesis — inputs, outputs and energy flow (concept map)", "blockId": "live_block_3_f73b60ae", "content": "{\\"schemaVersion\\":\\"petros.render.v2\\",\\"title\\":\\"Photosynthesis — inputs, outputs and energy flow (concept map)\\",\\"type\\":\\"diagram\\",\\"diagramType\\":\\"concept_map\\",\\"nodes\\":[{\\"id\\":\\"p\\",\\"label\\":\\"Photosynthesis (in chloroplasts)\\",\\"description\\":\\"Central process where light energy drives assembly of sugar from CO₂ and H₂O.\\"},{\\"id\\":\\"co2\\",\\"label\\":\\"Carbon dioxide (CO₂)\\",\\"description\\":\\"From air; provides carbon atoms for sugar.\\"},{\\"id\\":\\"h2o\\",\\"label\\":\\"Water (H₂O)\\",\\"description\\":\\"From soil; provides hydrogen and oxygen atoms.\\"},{\\"id\\":\\"light\\",\\"label\\":\\"Light energy (sunlight)\\",\\"description\\":\\"Energy input captured by chlorophyll; drives the reaction.\\"},{\\"id\\":\\"o2\\",\\"label\\":\\"Oxygen (O₂)\\",\\"description\\":\\"By-product released to the air.\\"},{\\"id\\":\\"glucose\\",\\"label\\":\\"Glucose / sugar (C₆H₁₂O₆)\\",\\"description\\":\\"Main product: stored chemical energy and source of carbon.\\"}],\\"edges\\":[{\\"from\\":\\"co2\\",\\"to\\":\\"p\\",\\"label\\":\\"carbon atoms →\\"},{\\"from\\":\\"h2o\\",\\"to\\":\\"p\\",\\"label\\":\\"hydrogen (and oxygen) atoms →\\"},{\\"from\\":\\"light\\",\\"to\\":\\"p\\",\\"label\\":\\"energy input →\\"},{\\"from\\":\\"p\\",\\"to\\":\\"glucose\\",\\"label\\":\\"makes / assembles →\\"},{\\"from\\":\\"p\\",\\"to\\":\\"o2\\",\\"label\\":\\"releases →\\"}],\\"altText\\":\\"Concept map of photosynthesis: central node labelled 'Photosynthesis (in chloroplasts)' connected from three input nodes: 'Carbon dioxide (CO₂) — from air' (arrow labelled 'carbon atoms →'), 'Water (H₂O) — from soil' (arrow labelled 'hydrogen (and oxygen) atoms →'), and 'Light energy (sunlight)' (arrow labelled 'energy input →'). Two output arrows lead from the central node: one to 'Glucose / sugar (C₆H₁₂O₆)' (labelled 'makes / assembles →') and one to 'Oxygen (O₂)' (labelled 'releases →'). The map highlights that atoms come from CO₂ and H₂O and energy comes from light.\\",\\"editable\\":true,\\"fallbackText\\":\\"Photosynthesis concept map (text): Inputs — Carbon dioxide (CO2, from air): provides carbon atoms; Water (H2O, from soil): provides hydrogen and oxygen atoms; Light energy (sunlight): provides energy. Central process: Photosynthesis (in chloroplasts). Outputs — Glucose / sugar (C6H12O6): assembled product; Oxygen (O2): released by-product. Arrows: CO2 → Photosynthesis (carbon atoms), H2O → Photosynthesis (hydrogen/oxygen atoms), Light → Photosynthesis (energy input), Photosynthesis → Glucose (makes/assembles), Photosynthesis → O2 (releases).\\"}", "startedAtMs": 50362, "completedAtMs": 50362, "sourceAnchors": [], "decisionFactors": ["Topic profile recommends a diagram after the goal_frame in the opening pattern.", "Learner is in a short revise session; a single focused visual move is efficient.", "Prior moves corrected the soil-food misconception and set a 5-minute outcome; a diagram supports quick application.", "Diagram suits the topic's high visual weighting and low mathematical load."], "artifactProposed": false, "expectedEvidence": "Learner labels the diagram correctly (identifies CO2, H2O, light, O2, and glucose or 'sugar' and indicates energy input) or uses the diagram to answer a 1-line application prompt about where the atoms and energy come from/ go to.", "firstContentAtMs": 50362, "studentSafeReason": "A visual diagram matches the topic profile's strong visual representation preference and helps a confidently-wrong learner organise corrected facts into a clear, low-effort mental model.", "studentConsentRequired": false}	2026-08-09 18:35:00.905796+03
