3505c7de-cc79-45b7-a212-e4597588ae71	392e146e-92d3-486c-a4f3-efa54a628127	eb73a6b6-6458-44c4-89f6-6a6f79259bb1	live_move_1_4431ce83	move	misconception_demolition	\N	{"id": "live_move_1_4431ce83", "type": "misconception_demolition", "phase": "active", "title": "Soil-food misconception", "blockId": "live_block_1_fc8418c0", "content": "{\\"schemaVersion\\":\\"petros.render.v2\\",\\"title\\":\\"Soil-food misconception\\",\\"type\\":\\"misconception_demolition\\",\\"misconception\\":\\"Plants take food directly from the soil (they 'eat' soil nutrients as food).\\",\\"commitmentPrompt\\":\\"Before I explain, do you agree or disagree with this statement: 'Plants get their food from the soil'? Type 'agree' or 'disagree' (or your short prediction).\\",\\"correction\\":\\"Correction: Plants do not take ready-made food from soil. They make their own food (glucose) inside their leaves by combining carbon dioxide (from air) and water (from soil) using energy from sunlight; minerals from soil help growth but are not the plant's food.\\",\\"evidence\\":[\\"Balanced summary equation: Carbon dioxide + Water + light energy → Glucose + Oxygen (shows carbon and hydrogen come from CO2 and H2O, not soil).\\",\\"Observation: Food-making structures (chloroplasts) are in leaf cells where light is captured, so the process occurs in leaves using sunlight rather than in roots taking in 'food' from soil.\\",\\"Role of soil minerals: Minerals from soil (like nitrogen, phosphorus) support growth and building tissues but are not the main chemical fuel (glucose) the plant produces.\\"]}", "startedAtMs": 15031, "completedAtMs": 15031, "sourceAnchors": [], "decisionFactors": ["Topic profile lists 'soil-food' as a high-priority misconception.", "Opening pattern recommends starting with misconception demolition to prevent wasted effort later.", "Session is a revise/evaluation session so explicit correction is appropriate.", "Requires a small, low-cost commitment from the learner before revealing correction."], "artifactProposed": false, "expectedEvidence": "Learner agrees to attempt a short prediction/commitment and, after the activity, can state that plants make glucose from CO2 and H2O using light energy (not by taking food from soil).", "firstContentAtMs": 15031, "studentSafeReason": "This move targets a common, high-impact misconception so the learner can build an accurate model of how plants make their own food.", "studentConsentRequired": false}	2026-08-09 18:35:00.905796+03
e6b7a68d-0d45-4427-b2c7-ff38afbfb4b4	392e146e-92d3-486c-a4f3-efa54a628127	eb73a6b6-6458-44c4-89f6-6a6f79259bb1	live_move_2_621cd0eb	move	goal_frame	\N	{"id": "live_move_2_621cd0eb", "type": "goal_frame", "phase": "opening", "title": "Set a short-term photosynthesis goal", "blockId": "live_block_2_eb559aa4", "content": "{\\"schemaVersion\\":\\"petros.render.v2\\",\\"title\\":\\"Set a short-term photosynthesis goal\\",\\"type\\":\\"goal_frame\\",\\"goal\\":\\"In one sentence, articulate a brief, timeboxed goal (within this 4-minute activity) describing what you will be able to explain or do about photosynthesis — for example: 'I will explain how plants make glucose from CO2, H2O and light.'\\",\\"timeEstimateMinutes\\":4,\\"checkpoints\\":[\\"Write a single clear sentence stating what you will be able to explain or do about photosynthesis.\\",\\"Ensure the sentence is timeboxed to this session (it should describe what you can achieve now or by the end of the next few minutes).\\",\\"If you can't produce a sentence, choose the suggested goal provided by the tutor and confirm you accept it.\\"]}", "startedAtMs": 30082, "completedAtMs": 30082, "sourceAnchors": [], "decisionFactors": ["Opening pattern in topic profile recommends goal framing after misconception demolition.", "Session is a short revise/evaluation with limited remaining moves — keep the goal timeboxed and actionable.", "Learner just completed a commitment; follow-up should ask for a short, explicit outcome to check engagement."], "artifactProposed": false, "expectedEvidence": "Learner states a brief, actionable goal (one sentence) such as 'I will explain how plants make glucose from CO2, H2O and light' or selects an equivalent single-sentence outcome.", "firstContentAtMs": 30082, "studentSafeReason": "Framing a concise, timeboxed outcome focuses the learner's effort after the misconception correction and sets an achievable commitment for the session.", "studentConsentRequired": false}	2026-08-09 18:35:00.905796+03
258e8c32-c729-46f3-89cc-d919cfb7531b	392e146e-92d3-486c-a4f3-efa54a628127	eb73a6b6-6458-44c4-89f6-6a6f79259bb1	live_move_3_3b60a275	move	diagram	\N	{"id": "live_move_3_3b60a275", "type": "diagram", "phase": "explaining", "title": "Concept map: Core inputs, processes and outputs of photosynthesis", "blockId": "live_block_3_170495dc", "content": "{\\"schemaVersion\\":\\"petros.render.v2\\",\\"title\\":\\"Concept map: Core inputs, processes and outputs of photosynthesis\\",\\"type\\":\\"diagram\\",\\"diagramType\\":\\"concept_map\\",\\"nodes\\":[{\\"id\\":\\"light\\",\\"label\\":\\"Light (energy)\\",\\"description\\":\\"Visible light from the sun; provides the energy that drives the reactions in the chloroplast.\\"},{\\"id\\":\\"co2\\",\\"label\\":\\"Carbon dioxide (CO2)\\",\\"description\\":\\"Gas taken from the air; a carbon source for making glucose.\\"},{\\"id\\":\\"h2o\\",\\"label\\":\\"Water (H2O)\\",\\"description\\":\\"Taken up by roots; provides hydrogen atoms and electrons and is split during the light reactions.\\"},{\\"id\\":\\"chloroplast\\",\\"label\\":\\"Chloroplast (site of photosynthesis)\\",\\"description\\":\\"Cell organelle where light energy is captured and used to assemble glucose from CO2 and H2O.\\"},{\\"id\\":\\"glucose\\",\\"label\\":\\"Glucose (C6H12O6)\\",\\"description\\":\\"Main organic product — a carbohydrate assembled from CO2 and H2O using light energy.\\"},{\\"id\\":\\"oxygen\\",\\"label\\":\\"Oxygen (O2)\\",\\"description\\":\\"By-product released when water is split during the light-driven reactions.\\"}],\\"edges\\":[{\\"from\\":\\"light\\",\\"to\\":\\"chloroplast\\",\\"label\\":\\"supplies energy to\\"},{\\"from\\":\\"co2\\",\\"to\\":\\"chloroplast\\",\\"label\\":\\"input (carbon source)\\"},{\\"from\\":\\"h2o\\",\\"to\\":\\"chloroplast\\",\\"label\\":\\"input (hydrogen/electrons)\\"},{\\"from\\":\\"chloroplast\\",\\"to\\":\\"glucose\\",\\"label\\":\\"uses energy to assemble ->\\"},{\\"from\\":\\"chloroplast\\",\\"to\\":\\"oxygen\\",\\"label\\":\\"releases (by-product)\\"},{\\"from\\":\\"co2\\",\\"to\\":\\"glucose\\",\\"label\\":\\"carbon atoms become part of\\"},{\\"from\\":\\"h2o\\",\\"to\\":\\"oxygen\\",\\"label\\":\\"source of O atoms released as\\"}],\\"altText\\":\\"Editable concept map showing light, CO2 and H2O as inputs to the chloroplast. Light supplies energy; chloroplast assembles glucose and releases oxygen. Arrows label inputs, energy flow, and products.\\",\\"editable\\":true,\\"fallbackText\\":\\"Concept map: Inputs — Light (energy), CO2 (carbon source), H2O (hydrogen/electrons). Central node: Chloroplast (site of photosynthesis). Outputs — Glucose (assembled from CO2 + H2O using light energy) and Oxygen (released when water is split). Edges label energy supply, inputs, product formation, and by-product release.\\"}", "startedAtMs": 51385, "completedAtMs": 51385, "sourceAnchors": [], "decisionFactors": ["Topic profile ranks visual representations (diagram) highly for Photosynthesis.", "Learner already completed misconception-demolition and goal framing; a diagram is the next recommended move in the opening->practice pattern.", "Remaining time and moves are limited — choose a concise, editable diagram activity to elicit evidence of understanding.", "Diagrams suit the low-mathematical, high-visual nature of the target concept."], "artifactProposed": false, "expectedEvidence": "Learner fills or edits the concept map to show CO2 and H2O as inputs, light as the energy source, and glucose (and O2) as products, and indicates the rough relationship (light energy used to assemble glucose from CO2 + H2O).", "firstContentAtMs": 51385, "studentSafeReason": "A visual representation helps consolidate the learner's corrected model after the misconception correction and goal framing; it's low-cost and matches the topic's high visual weight.", "studentConsentRequired": false}	2026-08-09 18:35:00.905796+03
