.\\",\\"predictionPrompt\\":\\"After seeing the first line, predict the full sequence of printed lines from the whole loop.\\"},{\\"id\\":\\"step-4\\",\\"title\\":\\"Quick variation to apply the idea\\",\\"explanation\\":\\"Variation: using range to loop over indices instead.\\\\n\\\\ncode:\\\\nfor i in range(3):\\\\n    print(i)\\\\n\\\\nThis prints 0, 1, 2 on separate lines because range(3) yields 0, 1, 2 in order. The loop variable takes each yielded value in sequence.\\",\\"predictionPrompt\\":\\"Predict the three lines this variation prints (one short line each or a single comma-separated line).\\"}],\\"finalCheck\\":\\"In one sentence, explain why the loop prints values in the order shown and what would change if the list order changed.\\",\\"sourceGrounding\\":[{\\"label\\":\\"Python official tutorial — for loops\\",\\"reference\\":\\"https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/controlflow.html#for-statements\\"}]}", "startedAtMs": 49968, "completedAtMs": 49968, "sourceAnchors": [], "decisionFactors": ["Topic profile recommends worked_example after predict_then_reveal for sequential skill-building", "Session time is limited (≈5 minutes remaining) so a focused procedural example is appropriate", "We need a cheap commitment (one short attempt/prediction) before revealing each step to check understanding"], "artifactProposed": false, "expectedEvidence": "Student follows the worked example and correctly explains or predicts the output for at least the intermediate step highlighted (one prediction point), demonstrating comprehension of iteration and loop body execution.", "firstContentAtMs": 49968, "studentSafeReason": "A short worked example demonstrates the mechanics of for-loops (iteration order, loop body execution, and variable updates) and lets the student practice with minimal cognitive load after the prediction step.", "studentConsentRequired": false}	2026-08-09 18:35:00.905796+03
