atement\\":\\"Walk through a short procedural example that uses a for-loop to compute a running total from a list of numbers. Predict one intermediate value, then follow the remaining steps to the final result.\\",\\"steps\\":[{\\"id\\":\\"step-1\\",\\"title\\":\\"Read the code\\",\\"explanation\\":\\"Consider this code: numbers = [2, 5, 3]\\\\ntotal = 0\\\\nfor n in numbers:\\\\n    total = total + n\\\\nAfter each loop iteration, total updates by adding the current element n to the previous total.\\",\\"predictionPrompt\\":\\"Before running the full loop, predict the value of total after the first iteration (after n=2). Write your prediction as a single number.\\"},{\\"id\\":\\"step-2\\",\\"title\\":\\"Apply the first iteration\\",\\"explanation\\":\\"If n is 2 on the first iteration, total starts at 0, so total = 0 + 2 = 2. This confirms the prediction if you wrote 2. Now continue through the remaining iterations: second iteration adds 5, third adds 3.\\",\\"predictionPrompt\\":\\"(No prediction needed)\\"},{\\"id\\":\\"step-3\\",\\"title\\":\\"Compute the final result\\",\\"explanation\\":\\"Continue updating: after second iteration total = 2 + 5 = 7. After third iteration total = 7 + 3 = 10. The loop finishes and total holds the sum of the list elements, which is 10.\\",\\"predictionPrompt\\":\\"(No prediction needed)\\"}],\\"finalCheck\\":\\"What is the final value of total? If you ran the steps mentally or in a Python interpreter, confirm that it is 10. Optionally, write the equivalent one-line expression using sum() that produces the same result.\\",\\"sourceGrounding\\":[{\\"label\\":\\"Topic profile — Python for-loops\\",\\"reference\\":\\"Stage 4 fixture curriculum: canonical worked example for loop iteration and accumulation.\\"}]}", "startedAtMs": 40532, "completedAtMs": 40532, "sourceAnchors": [], "decisionFactors": ["Topic profile recommends a worked example after a prediction if prerequisite knowledge is present", "Remaining time is short so a focuse