dge of Boolean conditions before learning Python for-loops to avoid confusion about loop guards and condition checks.", "studentConsentRequired": false}, {"id": "live_move_2_04a9c5c4", "type": "predict_then_reveal", "phase": "active", "title": "Predict output of a Python for-loop", "blockId": "live_block_2_015274d0", "content": "{\\"schemaVersion\\":\\"petros.render.v2\\",\\"title\\":\\"Predict output of a Python for-loop\\",\\"type\\":\\"predict_then_reveal\\",\\"prompt\\":\\"Consider this Python code:\\\\n\\\\nfor i in range(1, 5):\\\\n    if i % 2 == 0:\\\\n        print(i * 2)\\\\n    else:\\\\n        print(i)\\\\n\\\\nBefore you run it, predict the exact printed output (one value per line). Then give one short sentence explaining your prediction.\\",\\"reveal\\":\\"The printed output is:\\\\n1\\\\n4\\\\n3\\\\n8\\",\\"explanation\\":\\"The loop iterates i = 1,2,3,4. For each i, the code checks if i is even (i % 2 == 0): if true it prints i*2, otherwise it prints i. So i=1 prints 1; i=2 prints 4; i=3 prints 3; i=4 prints 8.\\"}", "startedAtMs": 30607, "completedAtMs": 30607, "sourceAnchors": [], "decisionFactors": ["Topic profile recommends predict_then_reveal as an early move for sequential representations", "Cheap commitment (prediction) quickly diagnoses understanding of iteration and loop body execution", "Short session timebox — a prediction question is fast to answer and to grade", "Prerequisite check completed, so we can move to applying knowledge in a concrete example", "Prediction reveals both conceptual errors (e.g. off-by-one) and procedural misunderstandings (e.g. indentation/variable updates)"], "artifactProposed": false, "expectedEvidence": "Student gives a short answer predicting the loop's printed output (or next value) and a one-sentence justification. Correct prediction + plausible justification indicates readiness to proceed to worked examples.", "firstContentAtMs": 30607, "studentSafeReason": "Asking the student to predict a short, concrete outcome gives a cheap commitment and reveals specific gaps in loop mechanics without long exposition.", "studentConsentRequired": false}, {"id": "live_move_3_0d3d4916", "type": "worked_example", "phase": "explaining", "title": "Trace a Python for-loop over a list (short worked example)", "blockId": "live_block_3_d61839c2", "content": "{\\"schemaVersion\\":\\"petros.render.v2\\",\\"title\\":\\"Trace a Python for-loop over a list (short worked example)\\",\\"type\\":\\"worked_example\\",\\"problemStatement\\":\\"Step through this for-loop and follow variable updates and printed output. Predict the printed output at the checkpoint, then continue the trace to the end.\\",\\"steps\\":[{\\"id\\":\\"step-1\\",\\"title\\":\\"Read the code and initial state\\",\\"explanation\\":\\"Code: numbers = [3, 5, 2]\\\\nsum_so_far = 0\\\\nfor n in numbers:\\\\n    sum_so_far += n\\\\n    print(n, sum_so_far)\\\\nInitial values: numbers is [3,5,2], sum_so_far is 0. The loop will visit each element in order and update sum_so_far by adding the current n, then print the pair (n, sum_so_far).\\",\\"predictionPrompt\\":\\"Before running the loop, predict what the first printed line will be (the values printed on the first iteration).\\"},{\\"id\\":\\"step-2\\",\\"title\\":\\"First iteration (prediction checkpoint)\\",\\"explanation\\":\\"On the first iteration the loop takes n = numbers[0] which is 3. sum_so_far becomes 0 + 3 = 3. The print statement outputs the current n and the updated sum_so_far.\\",\\"predictionPrompt\\":\\"Enter the exact text printed on the first iteration (format: n sum_so_far).\\"},{\\"id\\":\\"step-3\\",\\"title\\":\\"Finish the remaining iterations and result\\",\\"explanation\\":\\"Second iteration: n = 5, sum_so_far becomes 3 + 5 = 8, print outputs \\\\\\"5 8\\\\\\". Third iteration: n = 2, sum_so_far becomes 8 + 2 = 10, print outputs \\\\\\"2 10\\\\\\". After the loop sum_so_far is 10.\\",\\"predictionPrompt\\":\\"After confirming the first printed line, state the full sequence of printed lines for all iterations, in order.\\"}],\\"finalCheck\\":\\"Confirm you predicted the first printed line correctly and that the final sum_so_far is 10.\\",\\"sourceGrounding\\":[{\\"label\\":\\"Example code\\",\\"reference\\":\\"Small illustrative Python snippet showing sequential iteration over a list.\\"}]}", "startedAtMs": 48786, "completedAtMs": 48786, "sourceAnchors": [], "decisionFactors": ["Topic profile recommends worked_example after a prediction to consolidate mechanics", "Remaining time and one move left — a short worked example is efficient", "Prior prediction revealed readiness to practice procedural execution", "Worked example supports sequential representation of iteration"], "artifactProposed": false, "expectedEvidence": "Student correctly follows the worked example steps, predicts intermediate printed values at one prediction point, and completes one short practice trace without errors.", "firstContentAtMs": 48786, "studentSafeReason": "A worked example lets the student see loop mechanics (iteration order, variable updates, prints) with minimal cognitive load and gives a scaffolded task to practice safely.", "studentConsentRequired": false}], "runId": "petros-stage5-live-smoke-v1", "usage": [{"source": "evaluation", "retries": 0, "cacheHit": false, "modelKey": "gpt-5-mini-2025-08-07", "fallbacks": 0, "latencyMs": 9259, "requestId": "db496e4c-3f0d-4eb6-93ff-8d948a4f2c78", "costMicros": 2571, "functionKey": "teaching.next_action.select", "inputTokens": 21814, "providerKey": "openai-live-eval", "outputTokens": 834, "validationFailures": 0}, {"source": "evaluation", "retries": 0, "cacheHit": false, "modelKey": "gpt-5-mini-2025-08-07", "fallbacks": 0, "latencyMs": 4898, "requestId": "3a681c0b-e3f2-402d-9bcd-bb7995b7405a", "costMicros": 1397, "functionKey": "teaching.block.render", "inputTokens": 5893, "providerKey": "openai-live-eval", "outputTokens": 394, "validationFailures": 0}, {"source": "evaluation", "retries": 0, "cach