f boolean conditions before introducing for-loop control flow; ensures instruction matches prior knowledge.", "studentConsentRequired": false}, {"id": "live_move_2_1bc47c2b", "type": "predict_then_reveal", "phase": "active", "title": "Predict the for-loop output", "blockId": "live_block_2_415e9229", "content": "{\\"schemaVersion\\":\\"petros.render.v2\\",\\"title\\":\\"Predict the for-loop output\\",\\"type\\":\\"predict_then_reveal\\",\\"prompt\\":\\"Look at this Python snippet and briefly predict (one short sentence) what it prints. Commit to your answer before I reveal the result.\\\\n\\\\nfor i in range(3):\\\\n    print(i * 2)\\\\n\\\\nWhat does this print?\\",\\"reveal\\":\\"It prints three lines with the numbers 0, 2, and 4 — one per line, in that order.\\",\\"explanation\\":\\"range(3) yields i = 0, 1, 2. Each loop iteration prints i*2, so the printed lines are 0, 2, then 4. If you wrote your prediction differently, note whether you included order and line separation; those matter for exact output.\\"}", "startedAtMs": 24480, "completedAtMs": 24480, "sourceAnchors": [], "decisionFactors": ["Topic profile recommends predict_then_reveal after prerequisite_check", "Time budget is short (≈5 minutes) so a cheap commitment is preferred", "Prediction elicits student model and gives diagnostic evidence for next move selection", "Keeps cognitive load low while still testing application ability"], "artifactProposed": false, "expectedEvidence": "Student provides a short prediction of the output or effect of a short Python for-loop snippet (one or two lines) and briefly commits to their answer.", "firstContentAtMs": 24480, "studentSafeReason": "A low-effort prediction prompt will surface the student's mental model of for-loop behaviour without long explanation; good for short remaining time.", "studentConsentRequired": false}, {"id": "live_move_3_a9b61044", "type": "worked_example", "phase": "explaining", "title": "Worked example — simple Python for-loop", "blockId": "live_block_3_ab1f6e8a", "content": "{\\"schemaVersion\\":\\"petros.render.v2\\",\\"title\\":\\"Worked example — simple Python for-loop\\",\\"type\\":\\"worked_example\\",\\"problemStatement\\":\\"Walk through a short procedural example that uses a for-loop to compute a running total from a list of numbers. Predict one intermediate value, then follow the remaining steps to the final result.\\",\\"steps\\":[{\\"id\\":\\"step-1\\",\\"title\\":\\"Read the code\\",\\"explanation\\":\\"Consider this code: numbers = [2, 5, 3]\\\\ntotal = 0\\\\nfor n in numbers:\\\\n    total = total + n\\\\nAfter each loop iteration, total updates by adding the current element n to the previous total.\\",\\"predictionPrompt\\":\\"Before running the full loop, predict the value of total after the first iteration (after n=2). Write your prediction as a single number.\\"},{\\"id\\":\\"step-2\\",\\"title\\":\\"Apply the first iteration\\",\\"explanation\\":\\"If n is 2 on the first iteration, total starts at 0, so total = 0 + 2 = 2. This confirms the prediction if you wrote 2. Now continue through the remaining iterations: second iteration adds 5, third adds 3.\\",\\"predictionPrompt\\":\\"(No prediction needed)\\"},{\\"id\\":\\"step-3\\",\\"title\\":\\"Compute the final result\\",\\"explanation\\":\\"Continue updating: after second iteration total = 2 + 5 = 7. After third iteration total = 7 + 3 = 10. The loop finishes and total holds the sum of the list elements, which is 10.\\",\\"predictionPrompt\\":\\"(No prediction needed)\\"}],\\"finalCheck\\":\\"What is the final value of total? If you ran the steps mentally or in a Python interpreter, confirm that it is 10. Optionally, write the equivalent one-line expression using sum() that produces the same result.\\",\\"sourceGrounding\\":[{\\"label\\":\\"Topic profile — Python for-loops\\",\\"reference\\":\\"Stage 4 fixture curriculum: canonical worked example for loop iteration and accumulation.\\"}]}", "startedAtMs": 40532, "completedAtMs": 4053