oolean conditions) before teaching for-loops to avoid wasted explanation.", "studentConsentRequired": false}, {"id": "live_move_2_42c3b942", "type": "predict_then_reveal", "phase": "active", "title": "Predict the loop output", "blockId": "live_block_2_5cc0810f", "content": "{\\"schemaVersion\\":\\"petros.render.v2\\",\\"title\\":\\"Predict the loop output\\",\\"type\\":\\"predict_then_reveal\\",\\"prompt\\":\\"Before I show the answer, predict in one short line what this Python code prints:\\\\n\\\\nfor i in range(3):\\\\n    print(i)\\\\n\\\\nWrite the exact lines you expect to see, in order.\\",\\"reveal\\":\\"The code prints three lines:\\\\n0\\\\n1\\\\n2\\",\\"explanation\\":\\"range(3) produces the sequence 0, 1, 2. The for loop assigns each value to i in turn and print(i) outputs that value on its own line. There is no extra output because print defaults to a newline and the loop runs three iterations.\\"}", "startedAtMs": 30181, "completedAtMs": 30181, "sourceAnchors": [], "decisionFactors": ["Topic profile recommends predict_then_reveal as the next move after a prerequisite_check", "Session is short (5 minutes) so a quick prediction exposes misconceptions efficiently", "Requires a cheap commitment from the student before revealing the correct behavior", "Prerequisite check completed and student likely ready for a focused example-based reveal"], "artifactProposed": false, "expectedEvidence": "Student submits a short prediction (one-line or brief sentence) about what a simple for-loop will output for a provided code snippet; the prediction lets us see whether they understand iteration and loop body execution.", "firstContentAtMs": 30181, "studentSafeReason": "Asks the student to commit to a short prediction about for-loop behavior before explaining; low-risk, quick, and reveals specific gaps to target instruction given the short session.", "studentConsentRequired": false}, {"id": "live_move_3_1420a9ab", "type": "worked_example", "phase": "explaining", "title": "Worked example: simple Python for-loop over a list", "blockId": "live_block_3_620061d5", "content": "{\\"schemaVersion\\":\\"petros.render.v2\\",\\"title\\":\\"Worked example: simple Python for-loop over a list\\",\\"type\\":\\"worked_example\\",\\"problemStatement\\":\\"Walk through this for-loop that iterates over a list of numbers and prints a message each iteration. Predict the highlighted intermediate result before it is revealed.\\",\\"steps\\":[{\\"id\\":\\"step-1\\",\\"title\\":\\"Read the code and initial state\\",\\"explanation\\":\\"Here is the code we will step through. Python will take each element from the list in order and run the loop body for that element.\\\\n\\\\ncode:\\\\nitems = [10, 20, 30]\\\\nfor x in items:\\\\n    print('Value:', x)\\\\n\\\\nInitial state: items is [10, 20, 30]. The loop variable x is not set yet.\\",\\"predictionPrompt\\":\\"Before any iteration, what will the first printed line look like when the loop runs?\\"},{\\"id\\":\\"step-2\\",\\"title\\":\\"Predict the first iteration's output (commitment)\\",\\"explanation\\":\\"The loop starts by taking the first element of items (10), assigns x = 10, then runs the body print('Value:', x).\\",\\"predictionPrompt\\":\\"Write one short line showing exactly what the first print call will produce (for example: Value: 10).\\"},{\\"id\\":\\"step-3\\",\\"title\\":\\"Reveal and continue the remaining iterations\\",\\"explanation\\":\\"Reveal: The first printed line is `Value: 10`.\\\\n\\\\nAfter that, the loop proceeds to the next elements in order:\\\\n- Second iteration: x = 20 -> prints `Value: 20`.\\\\n- Third iteration: x = 30 -> prints `Value: 30`.\\\\n\\\\nWhen the list is exhausted, the for-loop ends and execution continues after the loop.\\",\\"predictionPrompt\\":\\"After seeing the first line, predict the full sequence of printed lines from the whole loop.\\"},{\\"id\\":\\"step-4\\",\\"title\\":\\"Quick variation to apply the idea\\",\\"explanation\\":\\"Variation: using range to loop over indices instead.\\\\n\\\\ncode:\\\\nfor i in range(3):\\\\n    print(i)\\\\n\\\\nThis prints 0, 1, 2 on separate lines because range(3) yields 0, 1, 2 in order. The loop variable takes each yielded value in sequence.\\",\\"predictionPrompt\\":\\"Predict the three lines this variation prints (one short line each or a single comma-separated line).\\"}],\\"finalCheck\\":\\"In one sentence, explain why the loop prints values in the order shown and what would change if the list order changed.\\",\\"sourceGrounding\\":[{\\"label\\":\\"Python official tutorial — for loops\\",\\"reference\\":\\"https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/controlflow.html#for-statements\\"}]}", "startedAtMs": 49968, "completedAtMs": 49968, "sourceAnchors": [], "decisionFactors": ["Topic profile recommends worked_example after predict_then_reveal for sequential skill-building", "Session time is limited (≈5 minutes remaining) so a focused procedural example is appropriate", "We need a cheap commitment (one short attempt/prediction) before revealing each step to check understanding"], "artifactProposed": false, "expectedEvidence": "Student follows the worked example and correctly explains or predicts the output for at least the intermediate step highlighted (one prediction point), demonstrating comprehension of iteration and loop body execution.", "firstContentAtMs": 49968, "studentSafeReason": "A short worked example demonstrates the mechanics of for-loops (iteration order, loop body execution, and variable updates) and lets the student practice with minimal cognitive load after the prediction step.", "studentConsentRequired": false}], "runId": "petros-stage5-live-smoke-v1", "usage": [{"source": "evaluation", "retries": 0, "cacheHit": false, "modelKey": "gpt-5-mini-2025-08-07", "fallbacks": 0, "latencyMs": 8701, "requestId": "f94bb722-fcbb-4c18-808a-3a2e186a109c", "costMicros": 2567, "functionKey": "teaching.next_action.select", "inputTokens": 21820, "providerKey": "openai-live-eval", 