nd ensures examples will be at the right level.", "studentConsentRequired": false}, {"id": "live_move_2_1ecb7f0c", "type": "predict_then_reveal", "phase": "active", "title": "Predict the output of a simple for-loop", "blockId": "live_block_2_59f9339f", "content": "{\\"schemaVersion\\":\\"petros.render.v2\\",\\"title\\":\\"Predict the output of a simple for-loop\\",\\"type\\":\\"predict_then_reveal\\",\\"prompt\\":\\"Look at this Python code. Before I show the result, write a one-line prediction of exactly what will be printed (including order and punctuation). Commit to a short answer first.\\\\n\\\\nfor i in range(3):\\\\n    print(i)\\\\n\\\\nYour one-line prediction:\\",\\"reveal\\":\\"The program prints three lines with the numbers 0, 1, and 2 in order. Exact printed output:\\\\n0\\\\n1\\\\n2\\",\\"explanation\\":\\"range(3) produces the sequence 0, 1, 2. The for loop iterates over those values and print(i) writes each value on its own line, so you see three separate lines showing 0, 1, and 2. This is a common off-by-one trap: range(n) stops before n, starting from 0.\\"}", "startedAtMs": 36333, "completedAtMs": 36333, "sourceAnchors": [], "decisionFactors": ["Topic profile recommends 'predict_then_reveal' as the next move after a prerequisite check.", "Prediction tasks are low-cost commitments that surface students' mental models of iteration.", "Time budget is limited, so a quick short-answer prediction is appropriate.", "Helps decide whether to follow with a worked example or a brief remediation."], "artifactProposed": false, "expectedEvidence": "Student submits a short answer predicting the printed output (or behavior) of a 2–4 line for-loop snippet; answer either matches the actual output or reveals an off-by-one/type/ordering misconception.", "firstContentAtMs": 36333, "studentSafeReason": "A short prediction task asks the student to commit to an answer before explanation, revealing misconceptions without exposing them to lengthy instruction.", "studentCon